        "original_id": "96586cd2-bd56-5a1d-948a-760163cf1868",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Topics: Thermoception, Mammals\n- Concepts: Heat sensors, Cold sensors, Mammalia, Endothermy\n- Anatomy: Neocortex, Hair, Middle ear bones, Mammary glands, Milk\n\nFacts:\n- Mammals are vertebrates in the class Mammalia, a clade of endothermic amniotes.\n- Mammals are distinguished from reptiles, including birds, by the possession of a neocortex, hair, three middle ear bones, and mammary glands.\n- Females of all mammal species nurse their young with milk secreted from the mammary glands.\n- Mammals have at least two types of thermoceptive sensors: sensors that detect heat above body temperature and sensors that detect cold below body temperature.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
